.TH PERSONALITY 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
personality \- kernel binary emulation subsystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/exec/perso/personality.h>
.PP
.B struct personality {
.br
.BI "    const char *" name ";"
.br
.BI "    void **" syscall_table ";"
.br
.BI "    const char **" syscall_names ";"
.br
.BI "    struct syscall_fmt *" syscall_fmts ";"
.br
.BI "    uint32_t " syscall_count ";"
.B };
.PP
.BI "extern struct personality " personality_native ";"
.br
.BI "extern struct personality " personality_linux ";"
.br
.BI "extern struct personality " personality_freebsd ";"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B personality
subsystem allows the kernel to execute binaries compiled for different operating systems by intercepting system calls and mapping them to the kernel's internal implementation. Each process is associated with a specific
.B personality
structure, which dictates how system calls are dispatched and traced.
.PP
The
.I personality
structure contains the following fields:
.TP
.I name
A human-readable string identifying the personality (e.g., "Linux", "FreeBSD", "Native"). Used for process listing and debugging.
.TP
.I syscall_table
A pointer to an array of function pointers. The index into this array corresponds to the system call number (e.g., the value in the EAX register upon interrupt 0x80).
.TP
.I syscall_names
An optional array of strings used by the syscall tracer (`strace`-like functionality) to print the name of the system call instead of just its number.
.TP
.I syscall_fmts
An optional array of
.B struct syscall_fmt
entry, defining the number and types of arguments for each system call. This allows the tracer to pretty-print arguments (e.g., displaying strings, pointers, or hex values).
.TP
.I syscall_count
The size of the
.I syscall_table
and associated metadata arrays.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR perso_native (9),
.BR perso_linux (9),
.BR perso_freebsd (9)
